{
  "dom_id": "step-q352419",
  "name": "Question 19",
  "problem": "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round your answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(6+6{sinh}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following double-angle formula for cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=1-2{sin}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the following double-angle formula for the hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x=1+2{sinh}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above formula as\n\n$$\n2{sinh}^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x-1.\n$$\n\nWe can now integrate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(6+6{sinh}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(2+2{sinh}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(2+\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x+x)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & =3(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =3(\\frac{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-{e}^{-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}}{4}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =3(\\frac{{2}^{2}-{2}^{-2}}{4}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\frac{45}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
